# {{Project}} Feature Orchestrator

## What This Does
Research → **gate** → build → QA ∥ security → live-verify → regression test → report.
Routes work to specialists, parallel where boundaries allow.

## Model tiering (think with the expensive tier, type with the cheap one)
- **{{high}}**: `{{analyst}}`, planning + gate (main thread), `{{security}}`.
- **{{low}}**: `{{builders}}`, `{{qa}}`, live-verify.
Set per agent frontmatter; don't override to the high tier across the board.

## Execution model (KEY RULE)
The main thread does **only** Phase 1 (spawn research), Phase 2 (gate), and Phase 7 (report).
**Everything between the gate and the report runs in subagents.**
- Main thread **never** edits code, **never** drives the browser, **never** greps source to "just
  fix it". A one-line fix routes to the owning specialist — inline edits spend the expensive
  context on cheap work.
- **The gate is the ONLY interactive checkpoint.** After go, run Phases 3→7 with no further
  prompts. Re-prompt only if a Phase-4 FAIL needs a scope decision agents can't resolve.

## Token hygiene
- Subagents write detail to `{{scratch}}/*.md` and return a **terse receipt + path** — never paste
  full files into orchestrator context.
- Use a read-only search agent for "where is X" fan-out.
- Keep plain language only for what the human reads: research memo, security report, final report.

## Phase 0 — Context check
`{{scratch}}/` exists? partial-update request → re-run only affected agents · new requirements →
archive to `{{scratch}}_prev/` and start fresh · absent → initial run. Read existing receipts
first to avoid duplicate work.

## Phase 1 — Research
Spawn `{{analyst}}`. It never touches product code; it returns the memo as its final message and
**you** write it to `{{scratch}}/research_{feature}.md`. Memo sections: prior art · does it make
sense (value vs effort, simpler alternatives, scope cuts) · design-time security · {{legal, if
applicable}} · affected trees · **BUILD / RECONSIDER / NEEDS-INFO** verdict.

## Phase 2 — GATE (main thread — must actually halt)
Summarise the memo, then ask go/no-go and **stop until answered**. RECONSIDER → revise scope or
end. **No code before approval.** On go: this is the last stop.

## Phase 3 — Specialists
Routing: `{{path}}` → `{{agent}}` · `{{path}}` → `{{agent}}` · `{{path}}` → `{{agent}}`.
- `{{a}}` runs in parallel with `{{b}}` freely — disjoint trees.
- **`{{b}}` and `{{c}}` must NOT run in parallel** — both write `{{collision file}}`. Sequence
  them, or give that pair isolated worktrees.
- Dependent work (contract needed before consumer) → sequence: `{{producer}}` first.
- **Join rule:** spawn all parallel agents in a **single message**, join all before proceeding.
  One retry per agent on incomplete output, else proceed and note the gap.
Give each agent: the task, files to read first, its receipt path, which other receipts to read.
Single tree, no boundary crossed → run just that one, skip QA.

## Phase 4 — Verify: QA ∥ Security (parallel)
Both are read-only over the same build — run them **concurrently** and join both:
- `{{qa}}` → `{{scratch}}/qa_{feature}_report.md` — contract/boundary checks. Skip only if exactly
  one tree and no cross-boundary call.
- `{{security}}` → `{{scratch}}/security_{feature}_report.md` — audit of the branch diff.
- {{`compliance-auditor` — conditional: only if the Phase-1 memo flagged legal surface or the diff
  adds dependencies.}}
**Then ONE combined fix loop:** collect the union of all FAILs, re-run the owning specialist(s)
**once** with everything together, then re-verify the affected boundary. Proceed when reports are
PASS (or WARNs accepted).

## Phase 5 — Live verify {{only if a runnable instance exists}}
Spawn a cheap-tier subagent to drive {{the UI/API at <url>}} and confirm the change works.
**Never on the main thread.** It returns a PASS/FAIL receipt + what it confirmed.

## Phase 6 — Regression test

### Feasibility triage — do this FIRST
- **Test REQUIRED** — observable behaviour at {{the e2e layer}}: {{examples}}.
- **Gated one layer down** — logic that cannot be deterministically observed there ({{examples:
  async/timing/internals behind a mock}}). Cover with unit tests and **say so** in the report.

Never fake an end-to-end test by mocking the boundary under test. An admitted gap beats a
vacuous test.

### Writing it
`{{test-owner agent}}` writes `{{test path pattern}}`. **Assert unconditionally** — no
`if (x.length) expect(...)`, no waits for requests the test never triggers. **Run it before
opening the change** — never defer to CI. Keep this sequential after Phase 5: live-verify can
change the assertions.

## Phase 7 — Synthesize & report
```markdown
## Feature: {name}
### Research verdict: {BUILD/…} — {one line}
### Changes: {tree} — {summary}
### QA: PASS/WARN/FAIL — {issues}
### Security: PASS/FAIL — {findings}
### Live verify: {what was confirmed}
### Regression test: {path, or "none — gated one layer down because …"}
### Remaining checklist: {gate command} · {scans} · {coverage}
```
FAILs → describe clearly, ask whether to fix before finishing.

## Constraints (hard — from CLAUDE.md)
- {{gate}} must pass; {{scans}} clean; {{coverage floor}}.
- {{frozen contract}} · {{frozen name}} · {{mocking rule}}.

## Error handling
- Missing receipt → read source directly, don't block.
- Conflicting interfaces between agents → surface in the report, don't silently pick one.
- One retry per agent on clearly incomplete output; else proceed and note the gap.
